es  it's really best to have both phenotypic  and molecular tests available then  testing can be tailored to the need  taking into account considerations such  as  urgency cost and whether the  carbopytamines target is known  or unknown expense  is an important factor in test selection  but so too is the consequence of not  detecting a cpo  sometimes it may be necessary to use  both phenotypic  and molecular testing pcr for a rapid  result from a clinical sample  and a phenotypic test if the pcr test is  negative  even if a pcr test on clinical sample is  positive  a culture followed by a phenotypic test  to identify the cpo colonies can be  important  to allow the identification of the  organism performance of an  anti-microbial susceptibility test  and for sending the isolate to a state  laboratory  what happens then if pcr and phenotypic  tests  disagree which is the more accurate  a phantypic test can be positive and a  pcr test negative  when an isolated cap of pen amaze is  outside the target range of the pcr test  for example serration mass systems  isolates sometimes  produce the sme carbopenemas which the  pcr tests cannot detect  you may need to send the isolate to a  reference laboratory  to resolve this type of discordance  what about the reverse if the pcr  test is positive and the phenotypic test  is negative  maybe the pcr result was the correct one  due to the cpo's carbopytami's  expression  being below the threshold of sensitivity  of the phenotypic test  in that case the phenotypic test is  falsely negative  but another possibility is that it was a  false positive pcr  test because it detected dna from  non-viable cells  okay here's an interesting one that i  just can't stop thinking about  two chrome id carber plates inoculated  with the same rectal swab  but one grew lots and lots of cpo  colonies  and the other was negative the story is  this  a rectal swab in transport medium was  received by a hospital laboratory  and tested by bd max checkpo