c testing is the best means of  determining if the carbopenium  resistance  is or is not caused by a cavapine mase  so if phenotypic carbopenamos testing  should be the first line test  for carbopen and resistance pseudomonas  and pcr  uses the follow-up test that will  identify  some but not all carbopentamizers of  pseudomonas isolates  with carbopenium resistant acinotobacter  approximately ninety percent will be  producing  an oxacaba panama's that is not  included in the big five and cannot be  detected by pcr  the most pragmatic way to deal with  these  is the same way we deal with staph  aureus  approximately ninety percent of staph  aureus isolates  produce a beta-lactamase so we just  assume that a clinical isolate  is beta-lactamase positive without  testing  it makes sense to do the same with a  senator bacteria  oxygen acetobacter are difficult to to  detect  it's easier to assume that the  carbopenium resistant isolates  produce an oxocarbipin amaze  rare isolates will produce other types  of carbopylases  probably more often a metallobeta  lactamase but occasionally a kpc  if you are sending your carbopam  resistant to synatobactor isolates to a  state lab  and they tell you an isolate had a  non-oxicabin  maze then you should start testing for  it  this slide and the next one are largely  based on cdc  and wha recommendations  why do surveillance detection of  asymptomatic colonization  is very important not just to prevent  transmission  but also because often colonization  precedes  infection the cdc and wha  recommend that testing for asymptomatic  colonization  should be do should be guided by local  epidemiology and risk  assessment populations to consider  screening include if a patient has  recently stayed in an institution  with endemic cpos or a hospital in  another country  or been previously colonized or has been  in contact with a colonised  or infected patient another indication  for initiating  surveillance is an unexpected cpo  detection  example the first detection of a cpo in  an institution  or a patient population or the first  detection of a new type of  carbopytamines  or a patient becomes positive 48 hours  or later after admission  also there have been cases at least here  in louisville  where a very gene test on a positive  blood culture  detected a kpc producing e coli  which was carbopenium susceptible and  therefore would  not have been considered a candidate for  carbopytamise testing  in addition to these indications make  sure you follow  any state guidelines  remember early detection is vital to  prevent  transmission be proactive  think about other opportunities for  screening and interventions  in the context of risk assessment and  surveillance testing  based on local epidemiology you may need  to screen admissions from long-term  acute care facilities nursing homes  admissions to critical  care environments such as icus  transplant units and so on  who are at risk and may need periodic  testing during their stay  quite prevalent surveys that involve  testing all patients in a unit  or ward can be effective to rapidly  evaluate the prevalence of cpos in  particular wards or units  this is especially so if previously  unrecognized cpo patients have been  housed  on certain wards or units or to rapidly  evaluate for additional transmission  during an outbreak  a single point prevalence survey might  suffice  if few or no additional cpo colonized  patients  are identified but if ongoing  transmission is occurring  it may be necessary to do more  remember prevalence is a  key performance indicator of ability  to limit the spread of cpos  cdc is big on testing for intestinal  carriage  this is aimed at preventing patient to  patient transmission  and not so much other types of  transmission  links to cdc and wha recommendations  are at the bottom of this slide  now here's one for the thinkers to think  about  while testing for intestinal carriage of  cpos is a major screening focus  