ibitor combination  ceftazidime  av bacterium how big is the cpa  problem cpos don't just occur in medical  institutions  they are now all over the place and they  and their resistance genes can be  readily transmitted  if we don't have effective interventions  to keep them from doing harm  if you work in a hospital be aware that  hospital waste  water can contain cpos  cpos can also be in the food chain  large scale intensive farming practices  such as crowded factory farming enhance  the opportunity for transmission  and birds eating contaminated foods are  very effective transmitters  as they can fly to different locations  and disperse cpos in their excreta  there are even cpos in remote arctic  regions  you may remember reports during the last  olympic games of cpo contamination of  the beaches and ocean at rio de janeiro  this was due to contaminated hospital  sewage being dumped in the ocean  antibiotic resistant pathogens are now  such a  serious global threat that the united  nations has designated  antimicrobial resistance as one of the  three crisis level health challenges  along with hiv and ebola  there are only three tools we have to  fight against cpos  effective antibiotic therapy antibiotic  stewardship  and infection control  cpo infections are typically treated  with antibiotic combinations  that may include older agents of high  toxicity  such as polymixins and aminoglycosides  of uncertain efficacy such as tiger  cycling  or the new beta-lactamase inhibitor  combinations  even with combination therapy some cpo  infections are untreatable and  resistance continues to escalate  there's a huge push for new antibiotics  to combat  increasing antibiotic resistance  but it's failing utilization of the new  beta-lactamase inhibitor combinations  is hampered by cost concerns and  emerging resistance  the big companies have ceased developing  antibiotics  two of the smaller ones molinta and  archaeagen  that developed some potentially useful  drugs have declared bankruptcy  and others are struggling financially  and may not survive beyond december  in short complacency about new drugs  overcoming the cpo problem would be a  major mistake  this would go against everything we've  learned from the past  about how bacteria continue to evolve  and become  increasingly resistant  so what about antibiotic stewardship  in addition to aims such as cutting  costs and protecting patients from  adverse effects  one of the aims of antibiotic  stewardship is to use  antibiotics judiciously to prevent  further escalation of resistance  by reducing the emergence of de novo  resistance  and reducing the antibiotic selection  pressure  that suppresses susceptible bacteria  thereby enhancing the spread of  antibiotic resistant pathogens  the intentions are excellent but  stewardship only works sometimes the  current status of stewardship  with respect to antibiotic resistance  was recently summarized by schweitzer  and colleagues  who looked into the question of whether  it was being done the right way  they concluded that we really don't know  because we don't have good data from  good studies  that can answer this question quoting  from their paper  the current status of stewardship is  undermined by the poor quality of  research  addressing both what specific  antimicrobial use interventions are  effective  and how antimicrobial use improvement  strategies  can be implemented into practice  so we can only place limited faith in  new antibiotics  and antibiotic stewardship to turn  around the cpo  problem they will work sometimes  but we need something more effective  what about infection prevention and  control  the purpose of infection prevention and  control is to prevent  transmission of pathogens our two main  infection control tools  are hand hygiene and laboratory  detection  disinfection is another tool  in preventing cpo transmission a high  level of compliance with good hand  hygiene  is essential around eighty percent  compliance seems to be associated with  good results  what about laboratory detection  as already mentioned if we don't have  microbiologists to detect cpos  we're working blind and patients will  not be treated appropriately  and cpos will continue to spread  microbiologists are key players  let's take a look at some european  microbiology data  this slide comes from a report showing  the importance of transmission  it was a european study analyzing the  genome sequences  of over resistant klebsiella pneumoniae  from patients in 244 hospitals  in 32 countries approximately a third of  the isolates were cpos  that produced in order of occurrence  kpc oxa48 like ndm  vim and imp carbopenemasers  the major finding was that almost 70  of the cpos belonged to four  clonal lineages and their derivatives  there were transmissions within and  between hospitals  with most transmissions being within  hospitals  that is the hospitals were not  controlling transmission  through not controlling transmission  they were making their own  problem also  cpos had higher transmissibility  than non-carbopythmis producing isolates  another highly predictable finding was  that antibiotic exposure  facilitated the spread of cpos by  reducing the spread of more susceptible  isolates  if you want a short version of the study  heist  highlights i suggest you go to stan  derezinski's commentary in infectious  diseases  in clinical infectious diseases it's  listed here on this slide  so transmission is a big part of the  problem  this makes preventing transmission a  target for intervention  this slide shows how spanish health  professionals  prevented transmission in a nosocomial  outbreak of cre  it's representative of a number of  reports where there has been significant  progress  in reducing the occurrence of cpos and  the infections they cause  in healthcare facilities through  interventions  such as contact precautions patient  isolation or cohorting  hand hygiene and environmental cleaning  in a five-month period they had 18 cases  14 of which were acquired after  admission  environmental testing found seven  percent of the samples to be positive  for the rooms involved hand hygiene was  initiated  and improved compliance from 36 percent  to 85  and there are no positive environmental  samples after hydrogen peroxide  decontamination  in summary having recognized the problem  through microbiological testing  the interventions that worked in this  outbreak were improved compliance with  hand hygiene  and environmental disinfection  microbiological screening the  microbiology laboratory can use  non-molecular and molecular tests to  detect  carriage of cpos  the non-molecular tests are typically  performed after the growth  of bacterial colonies on culture plates  this task can be assisted by the use of  selective differential media  such as chrome agars that can help to  distinguish possible cpos from other  organisms  some representative tests are listed  here  the rapper deck carver np and the  neo-rapid cab kit are manual tests  which detect carbopenium hydrolysis  and the phenix cpo detect is an  automated  mic-based test that detects  and classifies carbopenemasers according  to susceptibility  and inhibition profiles there are also  rapid immunochromatographic assays  such as the ng test carbon 5 that detect  and classify five carbopenemas types  known as the big five these  are carbopenemasers of the kpc ndm  oxford 48 like and vim families  and some but not all imp carbopen amazes  molecular tests vary in what they can  test  with some tests being suitable for both  colonies and direct testing of clinical  samples  ideally carbopenemy screening should be  capable of detecting  all types of carbopenomases  for this reason the non-molecular  screening approach  should focus on rapid phenotypic tests  that detect a wide range of  carbopenemias  sub-optimal tests are those requiring  overnight incubation  or having a limited target range  representative molecular screening  technologies  are shown in this slide our focus today  is on pcr  tests such as the expert carburetor  and the bd max checkpoint cpo  even though these have a limited target  range  comprising the big five this is the  cheapest  and most widely used molecular  carbopytamise detection methodology  microarray testing such as the  checkpoint ct  103xl test offer an extensive  target range but are expensive and more  suited  for for reference laboratory testing  rather than routine screening  for genomic testing there are various  next generation sequencing technologies  but these are not widely available in  clinical laboratories  so let's compare carrier detection by  rapid phenotypic  and pcr tests  this table shows some advantages and  disadvantages  of the rapid tests that can provide a  fast turnaround  time to detect or exclude  cpos from clinical samples  for target range the phenotypic tests  are clear winners  with the automated test having the most  extensive detection range  and the manual tests having good target  ranges but being  unreliable for detecting oxacaba pina  mazes  especially those produced by  synodobacter  the pcr tests can only target the big  five and can have problems with  detecting genes  and coding imp carbopenomases which may  be more common in pseudomonas aeruginosa  than in the enterobacterialis  false positive tests are an issue with  the phenotypic tests  but are rare for pcr tests  amc production and porin mutations  are the most common mechanisms  responsible for false positive  phenotypic tests  phenotypic pcr tests can be used for  direct testing of clinical samples  such as rectal swabs but phenotypic  tests cannot  they require growth of colonies on a  culture plate before the test can be  performed  the phenotypic tests are much cheaper to  do  and the manual phenotypic tests do not  require expensive equipment for the test  to be done  in summary each technology has  advantages and disadvantages  it's really best to have both phenotypic  and molecular tests available then  testing can be tailored to the need  taking into account considerations such  as  urgency cost and whether the  carbopytamines target is known  or unknown expense  is an important factor in test selection  but so too is the consequence of not  detecting a cpo  sometimes it may be necessary to use  both phenotypic  and molecular testing pcr for a rapid  result from a clinical sample  and a phenotypic test if the pcr test is  negative  even if a pcr test on clinical sample is  positive  a culture followed by a phenotypic test  to identify the cpo colonies can be  important  to allow the identification of the  organism performance of an  anti-microbial susceptibility test  and for sending the isolate to a state  laboratory  what happens then if pcr and phenotypic  tests  disagree which is the more accurate  a phantypic test can be positive and a  pcr test negative  when an isolated cap of pen amaze is  outside the target range of the pcr test  for example serration mass systems  isolates sometimes  produce the sme carbopenemas which the  pcr tests cannot detect  you may need to send the isolate to a  reference laboratory  to resolve this type of discordance  what about the reverse if the pcr  test is positive and the phenotypic test  is negative  maybe the pcr result was the correct one  due to the cpo's carbopytami's  expression  being below the threshold of sensitivity  of the phenotypic test  in that case the phenotypic test is  falsely negative  but another possibility is that it was a  false positive pcr  test because it detected dna from  non-viable cells  okay here's an interesting one that i  just can't stop thinking about  two chrome id carber plates inoculated  with the same rectal swab  but one grew lots and lots of cpo  colonies  and the other was negative the story is  this  a rectal swab in transport medium was  received by a hospital laboratory  and tested by bd max checkpoint cpo pcr  test and cultured by streaking on  chromagod  the pcr test was positive for kpc  and the crowmager grew lots of  cpo colonies that can be seen in the  plate on the left  as dark blue colonies on the plate lots  of them  there was also pale there were also  pale pseudomonas aeruginosa colonies on  this plate  the cpa was identified as citrobacter  freundion  rectal swab in transport medium was sent  to another laboratory  where next day it was lawn inoculated  using a hockey stick spreader the  resulting culture on the right  was negative and had heavy overgrowth  of the pseudomonas aeruginosa repeat  attempts  in the second laboratory to recover the  citrobacter frying di from the transport  medium  by both streaking and lawn inoculation  were negative  how do you explain the false negative  second culture  was it the extra day in transport medium  did the pseudomonas produced something  that killed the citrobacter fry in the  eye  this can happen if you ever tested  pseudomonas in modified hodge tests  you may have seen how some pseudomonas  isolates are inhibitory  and cause clearing or no growth in parts  of the e coli lawn  whatever it was in this case the  concerns arising  are that delays in processing rectal  swabs  may result in falsely negative culture  results  and also be aware that a positive pcr  result  when a culture is negative should not  automatically be regarded  as a falsely positive pcr result  now for some issues for the advanced  players what about screening  carbopenem-resistant isolates of  pseudomonas aeruginosa and aciditobacter  a lot of effort and expense can go into  screening them for carbopenomoses  and you may get few or no positives  some points to consider  most carbopenem-resistant pseudomonas do  not  produce a carbopyl other mechanisms  usually cause carbopenem resistance in  this organism  of those that are carbopytamized  positive  the carbopanimase may be one that is not  detected by marketed pcr tests  phenotypic testing is the best means of  determining if the carbopenium  resistance  is or is not caused by a cavapine mase  so if phenotypic carbopenamos testing  should be the first line test  for carbopen and resistance pseudomonas  and pcr  uses the follow-up test that will  identify  some but not all carbopentamizers of  pseudomonas isolates  with carbopenium resistant acinotobacter  approximately ninety percent will be  producing  an oxacaba panama's that is not  included in the big five and cannot be  detected by pcr  the most pragmatic way to deal with  these  is the same way we deal with staph  aureus  approximately ninety percent of staph  aureus isolates  produce a beta-lactamase so we just  assume that a clinical isolate  is beta-lactamase positive without  testing  it makes sense to do the same with a  senator bacteria  oxygen acetobacter are difficult to to  detect  it's easier to assume that the  carbopenium resistant isolates  produce an oxocarbipin amaze  rare isolates will produce other types  of carbopylases  probably more often a metallobeta  lactamase but occasionally a kpc  if you are sending your carbopam  resistant to synatobactor isolates to a  state lab  and they tell you an isolate had a  non-oxicabin  maze then you should start testing for  it  this slide and the next one are largely  based on cdc  and wha recommendations  why do surveillance detection of  asymptomatic colonization  is very important not just to prevent  transmission  but also because often colonization  precedes  infection the cdc and wha  recommend that testing for asymptomatic  colonization  should be do should be guided by local  epidemiology and risk  assessment populations to consider  screening include if a patient has  recently stayed in an institution  with endemic cpos or a hospital in  another country  or been previously colonized or has been  in contact with a colonised  or infected patient another indication  for initiating  surveillance is an unexpected cpo  detection  example the first detection of a cpo in  an institution  or a patient population or the first  detection of a new type of  carbopytamines  or a patient becomes positive 48 hours  or later after admission  also there have been cases at least here  in louisville  where a very gene test on a positive  blood culture  detected a kpc producing e coli  which was carbopenium susceptible and  therefore would  not have been considered a candidate for  carbopytamise testing  in addition to these indications make  sure you follow  any state guidelines  remember early detection is vital to  prevent  transmission be proactive  think about other opportunities for  screening and interventions  in the context of risk assessment and  surveillance testing  based on local epidemiology you may need  to screen admissions from long-term  acute care facilities nursing homes  admissions to critical  care environments such as icus  transplant units and so on  who are at risk and may need periodic  testing during their stay  quite prevalent surveys that involve  testing all patients in a unit  or ward can be effective to rapidly  evaluate the prevalence of cpos in  particular wards or units  this is especially so if previously  unrecognized cpo patients have been  housed  on certain wards or units or to rapidly  evaluate for additional transmission  during an outbreak  a single point prevalence survey might  suffice  if few or no additional cpo colonized  patients  are identified but if ongoing  transmission is occurring  it may be necessary to do more  remember prevalence is a  key performance indicator of ability  to limit the spread of cpos  cdc is big on testing for intestinal  carriage  this is aimed at preventing patient to  patient transmission  and not so much other types of  transmission  links to cdc and wha recommendations  are at the bottom of this slide  now here's one for the thinkers to think  about  while testing for intestinal carriage of  cpos is a major screening focus  what happens when cpo contaminated  excretor gets into hospital waste water  the cpos don't just sit there  they become amplified hospital waste  water contains antibiotics excreted in  urine and stool  creating an environment in which  susceptible bacteria are suppressed  and cpos multiply and transmit their  resistance genes to  other bacteria subsequent waste water  treatment will decrease  but not eliminate pathogens or their  genes  the contaminated wastewater and sludge  which is the particulate matter in  sewerage  are then released daily into rivers and  farmland  sludge farms these are farms with a lake  for dumping sludge  into and the lake may receive something  like  50 truckloads of sludge daily  in which the process of cpo  amplification continues  so what is this to do with screening  there are now many papers about cpos in  hospital wastewater  if you screen a hospital's wastewater  a positive screen may indicate current  or past cpo carriage by patients  and also indicate the risk to patients  through being splashed by contaminated  waste water  from sink drains and high pressure  flushing of hoppers  and toilets on the other hand  a negative screen of wastewater may  indicate there is not  a cpo problem in your hospital  it's something to think about if you're  interested  this slide has some links to studies in  this area  in conclusion there should be  no complacency about the future of the  cpo problem  without effective counter measures the  prevalence of cpo carriage of cpos  and of cpo infections with bad outcomes  can be expected to increase  of the three potentially effective  countermeasures  new anti-cpo antibiotics antibiotic  stewardship  and infection prevention and control  infection prevention and control is  currently the most effective  the most crucial components of infection  prevention and control  are effective hand hygiene and rapid cpo  detection  in this context microbiologists must  continue to be first-line responders  in 